    ("p", "The Overview in {fig:s_over} gathers the state of the selected field: current weather, the crop being grown, the area, and the next irrigation, which in this example is Wednesday 6 January, about 128 mm, in 53 days. The field card shows a crop-health reading of 0.49 (moderate cover)."),
    ("fig", "s_over", "figures/screens/overview.png", "Overview page with weather, crop, area, next irrigation and field card.", 6.2),
    ("p", "In {fig:s_fields} the boundary of the sample field has been traced on satellite imagery. The panel above the map reports 1.19 ha (2.94 acres), a perimeter of 438 m, 120 m by 100 m, and a long side running east to west. The profile on the right holds the soil texture (clay loam), pH 6.8, canal water and furrow irrigation."),
    ("fig", "s_fields", "figures/screens/fields.png", "Field mapping page with a traced boundary, computed measures and the field profile.", 6.2),
    ("p", "{fig:s_crop} shows the first crop card for a planting date of 14 November 2026. Sugarcane is a Good match with a score of 96. Its factor scores are 84% for temperature, 98% for rainfall and 97% for pH. The reasons state that the season temperature averages 24 °C against the crop's preferred 24 to 37 °C, that rain over the 405-day season is about 1,271 mm, and that the soil pH of 6.8 sits inside the preferred 5 to 8. The card adds that sugarcane is grown on 51% of the farmland in the nearby districts and that the farmer should expect to irrigate about 2,182 mm."),
    ("fig", "s_crop", "figures/screens/crop-plan.png", "Crop plan page with the planting inputs and the first of the five crop cards.", 6.2),
    ("p", "In {fig:s_water} the irrigation page reports no irrigation needed in the next 14 days for sugarcane at day 21 of a 405-day season. It shows that 16 of the 56 mm of easily available soil water have been used up, that the crop will use about 13.2 mm over the next seven days with no forecast rain, and the next dates, each with an approximate depth. Because no last irrigation date was entered, the page states that the schedule is based on an assumed history, and it offers calendar links and an `.ics` download."),
    ("fig", "s_water", "figures/screens/water.png", "Water page with the irrigation inputs and the schedule for one crop.", 6.2),
    ("p", "{fig:s_ndvi} shows the crop-health page for the same field. The latest usable scene gives a mean NDVI of 0.49, labelled moderate cover, rising by 0.07 since the scene of 1 November, with all of the field in the moderate class. The colour map highlights the field boundary, the trend chart shows five readings between mid-October and mid-November, and the notes repeat that NDVI does not diagnose a cause. The attribution to Copernicus Sentinel data is shown on the page."),
    ("fig", "s_ndvi", "figures/screens/ndvi.png", "Crop health page with the NDVI map, the current reading and the trend.", 6.2),
    ("p", "{fig:s_report} is the one-page report that the farmer can print or save as PDF. It collects the field profile, the boundary map, and the crop, irrigation and fertiliser plans."),
    ("fig", "s_report", "figures/screens/reports.png", "Printable field report.", 6.2),
    ("h2", "5.6 Discussion and limitations"),
    ("p", "The results support a modest claim. The plant classifier is a useful first filter when the farmer names the crop, and the cascade covers its weak spots with a second opinion and an explicit statement of uncertainty. The crop-ranking engine produces explainable, field-specific suggestions that beat a national list and random choice on held-out districts, though a list copied from nearby districts matches local practice better still. The irrigation and fertiliser engines follow published methods and reproduce hand calculations. The system as a whole runs on free services."),
    ("p", "Several limits should be kept in view. The plant test set has 252 photos, and per-class results rest on 4 to 15 photos each, so the figures are indicative. Eleven classes have no field validation. The classifier covers 38 classes of mostly temperate crops, so many crops that Indian farmers grow, such as rice, wheat or sugarcane, have no disease class, and the vision model must answer for them. The crop ranking measures agreement with local practice in data that end in 2015, and it takes past weather as the guide to the coming season. Irrigation depends on model weather, and the soil water-holding values are general figures, not a measurement of the farmer's soil. NDVI depends on crop stage and cannot diagnose a cause. The fertiliser doses are indicative national values, six of which were checked against a published guide. The translations are unreviewed. Finally, no field trial with farmers has been run, so the usefulness of the advice is untested; the interface and engines have been exercised by the developers and by automated self-checks, not by a user study."),

    ("h1", "CHAPTER – 6: CONCLUSION & FUTURE SCOPE"),
    ("h2", "6.1 Conclusion"),
    ("p", "Dhartisense shows that field-aware advice can be assembled from free public data and standard methods, and delivered by one small Python server to a phone. A farmer maps a field once and receives crop suggestions that combine published crop requirements with local weather and local practice, an irrigation schedule from the FAO-56 water balance, a fertiliser plan from soil-test numbers, a satellite reading of crop condition, and a photo check for leaf problems. The assistant answers questions in five languages with the field's context."),
    ("p", "The design choices that came from the literature held up in practice. Scoring against published ranges avoided training on a small table. Calibrating the plant classifier and fitting thresholds let the application know when to defer. Letting the farmer's observation override the model kept the irrigation advice tied to what is seen in the field. Keeping all numbers in deterministic engines kept the language model from inventing doses or dates."),
    ("h2", "6.2 Limitations carried forward"),
    ("p", "The limits listed in Section 5.6 stand: a small field-photo test set, unverified classes, regional data that end in 2015, general soil and fertiliser values, model weather instead of sensors, unreviewed translations, and no field trial. Third-party data licences are not fully settled, which is why the repository contains no datasets or model weights, and some services used (Open-Meteo, public geocoders, Esri imagery) carry non-commercial or fair-use terms that must be checked before a wider launch."),
    ("h2", "6.3 Future scope"),
    ("bul", ["Run a pilot with farmers and extension officers, measure whether the advice is understood and followed, and use the feedback to correct the engines.",
             "Replace the 2008 to 2015 district statistics with a current official source, and add yield records from partners so that ranking can be scored on outcomes and not only on practice.",
             "Collect consented field photos of the crops that the model lacks, including cereals and sugarcane, and add them with an unknown class so that the classifier can refuse photos outside its training.",
             "Add state-specific fertiliser doses and a link to Soil Health Card records, and let the farmer enter a measured soil water-holding capacity.",
             "Let a native speaker of each language review the translations, and add voice input and read-aloud for farmers who prefer not to type.",
             "Support offline use with cached field data, and add a sowing-and-harvest log so that each season's results can be compared with the plan."]),
